 the  media that putin just woke up one day in  a bad mood decided to you know conquer  some new territory i think it's  important to see the russian perspective  on this and this is that  after nato expansionism for the past 30  years russia said this is a threat to  their security for the past 15 years  they made it very clear that  ukraine's absorption into nato is an  existential threat so in other words  this is a red line and  we and that they will effectively risk  everything for this so it's all or  nothing it's a little bit like a united  states we're willing to obliterate cuba  in order to get the soviets out so i  think it's under important understand  this because  if russia sees this effectively as a  defensive war if you want or getting  nato away from its borders then they  can't retreat what would happen if  russia would go back pull his forces out  today with the mood we have in the west  nato would work moves directly in with  weapons and everything else into ukraine  fairly quickly and again this is  something that russia cannot do so i  think the russians are willing to absorb  an enormous amount of economic pain and  they're not going to back down because  again for them this is an existential  threat it's a red line so i don't see  any future for the sanctions uh i think  if we don't want to change you would  have to talk about some security  arrangements with russia  okay chris wiefer  putin has said that  russia's strong enough to withstand  sanctions and that this will end up  hurting the west in the form of higher  food and energy prices is he right  well we can already see that include the  price of commodities key commodities  energy but also other commodities that  russia is an important supplier of for  example wheat uh another soft  agriculture  products all of these prices are rising  so it is putting inflationary pressure  on the global economy and therefore that  will affect uh monetary policy uh  policies and will will affect the global  economy so he's right in that respect  but to what extent russia can withstand  the pain and how long it can withstand  clearly is is an open question russia as  as i said earlier has made a lot of  preparations  uh you know to be more resilient against  sanctions for example if we go back to  2013  uh russia was entirely dependent on oil  price needed 115 dollars a year to  balance its budget this year it'd be  less than 50 although it's been equal  back then russia imported 50 of its food  consumption mostly from europe or from  the west now russia is completely  self-sufficient so there's no risk of  any food shortages certainly basic items  on the shelves uh and at the start of  this year russia had built up  substantial financial war chest  630 billion  dollars the fifth highest in the world  and we also know that the central bank  has greatly dispersed those reserves so  even though the central bank is now  sanctioned and and reserves held in  western banks are sanctioning countries  that they can't access but a lot of  reserves have been moved to china and  moved elsewhere where they can access  them so in that sense putin is right in  saying that russia can withstand a lot  of pain because uh he he has  made the economy a lot more resilient to  banking system etc is a lot stronger  than it used to be but the critical  factor though to repeat is uh so long as  russia is able to export its key  commodities such as gas and other  materials and be paid for them so long  as there are some banks remaining in the  swift system then russia is earning by  our estimate at least 30 billion dollars  current account surplus every month  because imports have collapsed so the  current account surplus has gone up and  that's a substantial financial lifeline  which means that the government can  continue to fund employment subsidies  maintain domestic economic stability  fund social programs  etc pensions etc and then that means  this situation in russia not ideal it'