llows from that that  the motivations of putin of those around  him are in many senses material are  about their own access to wealth and the  security that that brings so targeting  that wealth  is important as you say though the way  the west has gone about it in the uk but  also europe and the us has been  pretty sloppy especially in the uk the  enormous delay in imposing sanctions  when they were obviously coming leads to  a lot of anecdotal accounts of yeah  money being  spirited away before names right to the  sanctions list further to that the uk's  record of enforcing sanctions is  absolutely pitiful it remains the case  that the uk like the rest of the world  continues to allow this insane idea of  financial secrecy which has become so  widespread we've become accustomed to it  but you can if you're rich enough  participate in a democratic  free market economy in disguise behind a  front company so you put a few names on  the list and you maybe cause trouble for  chelsea and you grab a few yachts but we  don't know what the russian kleptocracy  is really doing with it with its wealth  because we allow them to move it around  in secret the the final point i'd make  and it picks up on  something that was raised earlier is  that is the prospect that this could  backfire by accelerating what i called  in my book the rise of kleptopia right  and alliances of kleptocrats by pushing  the russian kleptocratic elite but also  the  economy more generally  into um alliances with other um  ultra-corrupt states china venezuela  iran they all wear different kind of  ideological masks but essentially  they're all the political systems are  all  engines for the enrichments of the  powerful and the more we use sanctions  donald trump used them incredibly  liberally the more we use them the  bigger the club of outcasts get and the  more that club of outcasts band together  and if you throw everyone out then  sanctions ultimately become pointless  because there's an alternati